A||N||1||87ECO5||Belt replacement on dual-door cassette decks||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||06-30-2000||01:10 PM||||The factory cassette deck in my coupe won't play tapes.  The suggestion from most is to replace the belt.  OK - no problem.<P>I go to Rodney Dickman's site ($5 is better than $15+ from The Fiero Store) and get concerned after seeing this:<P>GM Delco Tape Player Drive Belts:<P>Typically the tape deck drive belt needs to be replaced every 3 years or so depending on usage. There are 2 types of tape decks used in the Fiero. The single door type and the dual door type. Specify which type you have when ordering. <B><I>Note: According to a reputable Delco repair center the dual door tape decks should be serviced by a professional. If you feel you are able to install your own drive belt I do stock them. There are 3(?) belts in the dual door tape decks. The drive belt is the only belt that generally needs to be replaced of the 3.</I></B><P>So, any of you guys ever worked on the dual-door cassette decks?  How much harder is it to replace these belts?<P>TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||Coconut||06-30-2000||10:43 PM||||87ECO5,<P>Yes... I have the dual door cassette deck and I did replace the main drive belt. Not a problem at all. All you need to do is follow the instructions exactly and you're set. Just take your time and make sure you have the proper tools to make the job easier(you'll need a very small set of phillips and slot head drivers). It took me about 15-20 mins. Not including the time to pull the deck from the dash. Hey, if I can do it anybody can!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Rest assured, you'll wonder what all the hoopla was about having it serviced by a professional. If you have any other questions, feel free to e-mail me.<P>Cruisin' On,<P>Coconut <P>BTW - While you're replacing the belt, it would be a good time to clean the tape head and rollers thoroughly.<p>[This message has been edited by Coconut (edited 06-30-2000).]||||reg||1
